WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:04.333
-Hi, everyone.
I'm Sarah Jenks-Daly,

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:06.375
supervising producer
at "Late Night,"

00:00:06.375 --> 00:00:08.458
and this is "Late Night Lit."

00:00:08.458 --> 00:00:14.958
♪♪

00:00:14.958 --> 00:00:17.875
Few improv groups have fostered
as much talent

00:00:17.875 --> 00:00:19.583
as that of Boom Chicago,

00:00:19.583 --> 00:00:22.083
which has been
entertaining audiences abroad

00:00:22.083 --> 00:00:24.083
for more than three decades.

00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:26.083
On this episode
of "Late Night Lit,"

00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:29.208
we're joined by Andrew Moskos
and Pep Rosenfeld,

00:00:29.208 --> 00:00:32.667
the co-founders of Boom Chicago,
who have written a new book

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:36.625
about their experiences,
entitled "Boom Chicago Presents

00:00:36.625 --> 00:00:39.875
the 30 Most Important Years
in Dutch History."

00:00:39.875 --> 00:00:41.333
It also contains a foreword

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:43.542
written by our very own
Seth Meyers.

00:00:43.542 --> 00:00:45.917
We're joined today
by all three

00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:48.417
to discuss
the improv group's origins,

00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:50.583
how Seth found his way
to Amsterdam,

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:53.292
and some of the stories
that didn't make the book.

00:00:53.292 --> 00:00:56.833
Now, here's my conversation
with Andrew, Pep, and Seth.

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:01.750
Hi, everyone. I am so happy
to be joined today

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.542
by three special guests.

00:01:03.542 --> 00:01:06.792
First up, two authors of a new
book entitled "Boom Chicago

00:01:06.792 --> 00:01:10.125
Presents the 30 Most Important
Years in Dutch History."

00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:12.625
First, we have Andrew Moskos.
-Hello.

00:01:12.625 --> 00:01:14.708
-And Pep Rosenfeld.
-Hi.

00:01:14.708 --> 00:01:16.375
It's great to be here.
Thanks.

00:01:16.375 --> 00:01:18.750
-And a man our listeners
might be vaguely familiar with,

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.167
Seth Meyers,
who wrote the foreword

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:22.000
for this very entertaining
oral history,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.083
which will be published in July.
Welcome back, Seth.

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.625
-Ooh, "vaguely."
Don't mind if I do.

00:01:26.625 --> 00:01:28.083
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.083 --> 00:01:29.875
-I wanted to ask first,
Pep and Andrew,

00:01:29.875 --> 00:01:31.333
how many notes did you give Seth

00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:33.208
on the first draft
of his foreword?

00:01:33.208 --> 00:01:36.167
-I think the deal was "he will
write it, but no notes."

00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:38.667
And that was a fair deal.

00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:41.583
-I think it also -- I would have
happily let them make --

00:01:41.583 --> 00:01:43.542
They could have changed
whatever they wanted

00:01:43.542 --> 00:01:46.125
and I would not have
probably noticed.

00:01:46.125 --> 00:01:48.042
-'Cause you're not
gonna read it?

00:01:48.042 --> 00:01:50.542
-I'm not gonna read
my own foreword.

00:01:50.542 --> 00:01:53.542
-The book covers 30 years
of the history of Boom Chicago,

00:01:53.542 --> 00:01:56.375
which you guys co-founded
in Amsterdam in the early '90s.

00:01:56.375 --> 00:01:57.958
I want to talk about the book

00:01:57.958 --> 00:01:59.583
and what it was like
to relive all these stories.

00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:01.250
But first,
putting the book together.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.667
Whose idea was this?

00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:04.875
Was it more joyful
than you anticipated?

00:02:04.875 --> 00:02:06.708
More arduous?
What was the process like?

00:02:06.708 --> 00:02:08.417
-It was kind of joyful,
I would say.

00:02:08.417 --> 00:02:10.375
-It was both joyful and arduous,
but I would say

00:02:10.375 --> 00:02:12.250
it's definitely more joyful.
-Yeah.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.458
So, we had a friend of ours
from high school,

00:02:14.458 --> 00:02:16.125
Matt Diehl,
who's also an author,

00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:18.667
and he has been pushing us
for years to write a book.

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:20.542
So, it's certainly his idea,

00:02:20.542 --> 00:02:22.917
but the process of writing it
was a lot of fun.

00:02:22.917 --> 00:02:26.125
I mean, we are writers
ourselves, so it's --

00:02:26.125 --> 00:02:28.667
writing a book
was just a small step.

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:30.958
-And it's funny 'cause Matt did
most of the interviews

00:02:30.958 --> 00:02:32.375
'cause a big part of the book

00:02:32.375 --> 00:02:34.667
is sort of an oral history
of Boom Chicago,

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:37.458
you know, told in the words
of a lot of alumni,

00:02:37.458 --> 00:02:41.625
including Seth,
and Matt did those interviews.

00:02:41.625 --> 00:02:43.083
But then it was
such a delight for us

00:02:43.083 --> 00:02:44.750
to read the interviews
afterwards

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.542
and hear what people said
about their Boom experience.

00:02:46.542 --> 00:02:48.042
It was great.

00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:50.083
-Seth is one of the many
people you interviewed.

00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:52.042
When you asked people
to participate,

00:02:52.042 --> 00:02:53.792
did you ask them
or did Matt ask them

00:02:53.792 --> 00:02:55.417
to talk about
certain subjects?

00:02:55.417 --> 00:02:57.458
Like, how did that...?
And Seth, you can chime in, too.

00:02:57.458 --> 00:02:59.250
How did that
interview process go

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.833
from all of your perspectives?

00:03:00.833 --> 00:03:02.500
-I'm not gonna lie, I'm curious

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.458
what Seth's point of view was,
how it went down for him.

00:03:05.458 --> 00:03:07.125
-Yeah, well, so Matt,

00:03:07.125 --> 00:03:08.875
who I've been lucky enough
to know for a long time...

00:03:08.875 --> 00:03:10.792
And I should clarify
for our listeners.

00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:12.958
So, Boom Chicago is a place
I went right after college,

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:14.375
and I spent two years
in Amsterdam

00:03:14.375 --> 00:03:15.958
working at this
improv comedy theater.

00:03:15.958 --> 00:03:17.958
It really was the beginning
of everything for me

00:03:17.958 --> 00:03:20.417
and an incredibly essential
part of my growth.

00:03:20.417 --> 00:03:22.000
And I got to meet
all these amazing people,

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.667
including Pep and Andrew.

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:25.375
And so, Matt reached out
and said,

00:03:25.375 --> 00:03:28.208
"These guys are gonna
want to make this book

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:30.750
for the 25-year anniversary."

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.000
And I will say Matt was lovely
when he asked specific things --

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.750
"Do you remember this?
Do you remember that?"

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:37.292
It was so fun to talk
about them.

00:03:37.292 --> 00:03:40.417
He did have a few too many,
"I feel"...

00:03:40.417 --> 00:03:43.875
"My thesis is Boom Chicago
changed not just comedy,

00:03:43.875 --> 00:03:46.083
but drama for the world."

00:03:46.083 --> 00:03:48.375
And I would say,
"Alright, Matt."

00:03:48.375 --> 00:03:51.375
It was a very...
It had a lot of influence.

00:03:51.375 --> 00:03:55.417
But, you know, let's be a little
careful how we frame it all.

00:03:55.417 --> 00:03:57.208
-Right, I was worried
he was gonna be like,

00:03:57.208 --> 00:03:59.042
"Would you say, Seth,
that without Boom Chicago,

00:03:59.042 --> 00:04:01.125
you wouldn't be funny at all?"
Like, no, don't ask him that.

00:04:01.125 --> 00:04:02.625
He's a very funny fella.

00:04:02.625 --> 00:04:04.667
-I think that the really
impressive thing,

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:06.417
and what I think
I would hope

00:04:06.417 --> 00:04:08.083
readers would take away
from this book

00:04:08.083 --> 00:04:12.208
is how it was a really
small organization

00:04:12.208 --> 00:04:15.500
that was filled with people
who went on to do big things.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.917
It didn't have the
infrastructure of a Second City

00:04:19.917 --> 00:04:23.292
and it didn't have the sort
of cultural significance

00:04:23.292 --> 00:04:25.417
of, say, an "SNL."

00:04:25.417 --> 00:04:27.250
It was really a small theater
in Amsterdam.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.917
And, you know,
over the course of 30 years,

00:04:28.917 --> 00:04:30.750
a lot of people have left
and done things

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.333
that, you know,
I would say at this point,

00:04:33.333 --> 00:04:34.792
the whole world knows.

00:04:34.792 --> 00:04:36.250
Not that
"Late Night With Seth Meyers"

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.042
has much of
an international footprint,

00:04:38.042 --> 00:04:40.125
but certainly, you know,
the films of Jordan Peele

00:04:40.125 --> 00:04:42.417
or a show like "Ted Lasso"
certainly do.

00:04:42.417 --> 00:04:44.083
-You do have a bit of appeal.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:47.417
You're sort of an Amsterdam
adjunct celebrity

00:04:47.417 --> 00:04:49.583
because you have
a passion for Amsterdam,

00:04:49.583 --> 00:04:52.500
so your stuff gets covered here
in the Dutch press, actually.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.417
-It's very nice when I get
a tiny side clipping

00:04:56.417 --> 00:04:59.125
from one of
the Dutch newspapers.

00:04:59.125 --> 00:05:02.250
-The clippings are big.
The newspaper itself is tiny.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.125
-That's true.
-Yeah, I was just gonna say,

00:05:04.125 --> 00:05:06.458
I think also, in contrast
with other theaters,

00:05:06.458 --> 00:05:09.625
because Pep and I have been
so active from the beginning,

00:05:09.625 --> 00:05:11.292
and Saskia, our third partner,

00:05:11.292 --> 00:05:13.875
we have our...

00:05:13.875 --> 00:05:15.375
you know, we're close
to the actors.

00:05:15.375 --> 00:05:16.833
We were in the show ourselves

00:05:16.833 --> 00:05:18.208
five days a week
for a long time.

00:05:18.208 --> 00:05:20.125
So, we don't just have
sort of like a,

00:05:20.125 --> 00:05:22.125
"Oh, we own the theater
and they're the actors"

00:05:22.125 --> 00:05:24.792
relationship --
we've been in the trenches.

00:05:24.792 --> 00:05:27.292
And I think that's why we have
such good friendships

00:05:27.292 --> 00:05:29.125
with a lot of our actors
as well, too,

00:05:29.125 --> 00:05:32.667
because there's no other theater
where the creative team

00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:36.042
is the owners and the creative
team themselves are onstage.

00:05:36.042 --> 00:05:37.833
-Well, the thing that
I'll say about that --

00:05:37.833 --> 00:05:39.500
it is a total parallel
to people who say,

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:42.625
"Who would take over
for Lorne when he leaves 'SNL'?"

00:05:42.625 --> 00:05:44.083
And I do think people forget

00:05:44.083 --> 00:05:46.167
that sometimes
these institutions survive

00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:48.667
because there is someone
who runs them

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:51.000
that has the underlying taste

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.333
that has been the thread
that has made it

00:05:54.333 --> 00:05:56.208
a consistent place

00:05:56.208 --> 00:05:57.875
for, in your case, 30 years,

00:05:57.875 --> 00:05:59.625
in Lorne's case, 50 years.

00:05:59.625 --> 00:06:02.083
But, you know, obviously,
there are gonna be levels

00:06:02.083 --> 00:06:04.167
where the show
is maybe better than it was,

00:06:04.167 --> 00:06:06.667
and then it just goes --
there's peaks and valleys.

00:06:06.667 --> 00:06:08.542
But I think if you don't have
one person who runs it,

00:06:08.542 --> 00:06:12.458
then it's -- you know,
it stops being unique.

00:06:12.458 --> 00:06:14.333
-Well, I think that
comes through in the book,

00:06:14.333 --> 00:06:15.958
certainly as a reader of it,

00:06:15.958 --> 00:06:17.625
and not knowing
any of the history.

00:06:17.625 --> 00:06:20.333
But I'd love to start with
or get on with the idea

00:06:20.333 --> 00:06:23.375
for Boom Chicago, which came
from the two of you guys,

00:06:23.375 --> 00:06:26.292
young men traveling
through Europe after graduation,

00:06:26.292 --> 00:06:29.625
slightly maybe influenced
by some substances.

00:06:29.625 --> 00:06:31.292
Can you kind of set
the scene for people

00:06:31.292 --> 00:06:33.708
as to just how
this all transpired?

00:06:33.708 --> 00:06:36.375
-Yeah, well, we always
loved Amsterdam.

00:06:36.375 --> 00:06:39.125
We were traveling,
doing a European tour.

00:06:39.125 --> 00:06:41.292
And when we came
to Amsterdam,

00:06:41.292 --> 00:06:43.625
we fell in love with the city
and we wanted to move there.

00:06:43.625 --> 00:06:46.333
And no, it's not
for the reason you think.

00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:48.000
It was for the drugs.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.667
And we actually were high
in a coffee shop

00:06:50.667 --> 00:06:52.167
and had the best
stoner idea ever,

00:06:52.167 --> 00:06:54.583
which was quit our jobs,
move to Amsterdam,

00:06:54.583 --> 00:06:57.042
and start an English-language
comedy show.

00:06:57.042 --> 00:06:58.875
And it worked.
-Yeah.

00:06:58.875 --> 00:07:01.583
I just want to say
that I think the key was

00:07:01.583 --> 00:07:04.583
that we had a pen with us
and wrote down the idea.

00:07:04.583 --> 00:07:07.917
So like, to any young people out
there considering doing drugs,

00:07:07.917 --> 00:07:09.667
I would just say,
yeah, do the drugs,

00:07:09.667 --> 00:07:11.583
but just make sure
you write your ideas down.

00:07:11.583 --> 00:07:13.792
Because half of them
will be terrible,

00:07:13.792 --> 00:07:15.250
one quarter will be illegible,

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:16.750
but one quarter might just
be something.

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.708
-In Dutch --
We've got these coasters

00:07:18.708 --> 00:07:20.417
in Holland --
like, they're thick.

00:07:20.417 --> 00:07:22.625
It's not like cocktail napkins
that goes under a drink.

00:07:22.625 --> 00:07:24.917
So, it's great
for writing down ideas.

00:07:24.917 --> 00:07:27.208
-The amount of times
in the two years I was there

00:07:27.208 --> 00:07:28.708
that Andrew said,
"I have a great idea"

00:07:28.708 --> 00:07:30.917
and it was on the back
of one of these, like...

00:07:30.917 --> 00:07:32.958
I guess like cork-y, almost.

00:07:32.958 --> 00:07:36.708
They feel like
a Heineken cork coaster

00:07:36.708 --> 00:07:38.417
that he would turn over,
and I'm like, "You know,

00:07:38.417 --> 00:07:40.417
it doesn't actually sell
as a great idea

00:07:40.417 --> 00:07:42.417
when you're flipping
over a beer coaster.

00:07:42.417 --> 00:07:45.583
I clearly know
where you got this idea."

00:07:45.583 --> 00:07:48.375
I should also say I do...
'Cause I feel like

00:07:48.375 --> 00:07:50.250
when people hear, like,
"We quit our jobs"...

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:53.125
Like, they were barely jobs.

00:07:53.125 --> 00:07:55.417
[ Laughter ]

00:07:55.417 --> 00:07:57.958
-Seth, I was an early childhood
literacy specialist

00:07:57.958 --> 00:07:59.625
at a corporate nonprofit.

00:07:59.625 --> 00:08:02.542
-Seth, I was an early evening
Italian food waiter.

00:08:02.542 --> 00:08:04.625
[ Laughter ]

00:08:04.625 --> 00:08:06.375
-Well, when you guys sobered up

00:08:06.375 --> 00:08:08.375
and approached the Dutch
government about this --

00:08:08.375 --> 00:08:10.042
you talk about this story
in the book --

00:08:10.042 --> 00:08:12.750
and the tourism board, they were
not exactly supportive,

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:14.250
I think is an appropriate word.

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:17.167
-What's the opposite
of supportive?

00:08:17.167 --> 00:08:19.333
-So, yeah, we wrote them
and they...

00:08:19.333 --> 00:08:21.083
We said, "Hey, here's our idea.

00:08:21.083 --> 00:08:22.792
We want to open up
a comedy show.

00:08:22.792 --> 00:08:26.208
We want it to be good enough
that it gets reviewed

00:08:26.208 --> 00:08:28.625
in the arts pages
of newspapers."

00:08:28.625 --> 00:08:31.292
Newspapers were a thing
that used to come out every day.

00:08:31.292 --> 00:08:32.667
-But you still have them, right?

00:08:32.667 --> 00:08:34.917
There's still a robust
newspaper scene,

00:08:34.917 --> 00:08:37.417
still robust newspapers
in Holland, right?

00:08:37.417 --> 00:08:39.292
-Yeah, actually,
they are still robust.

00:08:39.292 --> 00:08:43.375
Ten daily newspapers still,
and so it's a literate place.

00:08:43.375 --> 00:08:45.667
They get thinner.
They got thinner over the years.

00:08:45.667 --> 00:08:48.375
But, so we wrote the city
and the tourism office

00:08:48.375 --> 00:08:50.458
and said, "Here's our idea.
What do you think?"

00:08:50.458 --> 00:08:53.500
And they they wrote us back
quickly, by fax,

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.542
because it was 1993.

00:08:55.542 --> 00:08:58.667
And the fax said,
"Your idea will not work,"

00:08:58.667 --> 00:09:02.417
which is a pretty pessimistic
start for a tourism office.

00:09:02.417 --> 00:09:03.875
-But they went further.

00:09:03.875 --> 00:09:05.625
They fleshed it out.
-Oh, they fleshed it out.

00:09:05.625 --> 00:09:08.042
"The Dutch do not want
to see a show in English.

00:09:08.042 --> 00:09:10.417
The tourists do not want
to see a show at all.

00:09:10.417 --> 00:09:12.708
You will need subsidy
to theater in the Netherlands.

00:09:12.708 --> 00:09:14.375
You will not get any subsidy.

00:09:14.375 --> 00:09:16.542
So, think twice
about your plans."

00:09:16.542 --> 00:09:18.625
So, well, they were right about
we never got subsidy.

00:09:18.625 --> 00:09:20.417
-To be fair, I think....

00:09:20.417 --> 00:09:22.458
Does it count as subsidies,
the help we got during COVID?

00:09:22.458 --> 00:09:24.000
'Cause then that would be

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.125
the final domino of that letter
falling over.

00:09:26.125 --> 00:09:28.042
-But, no, we came.
It worked.

00:09:28.042 --> 00:09:30.500
We framed the fax, and it's up
in our bar right now.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.292
And 30 years later,
still having fun.

00:09:33.292 --> 00:09:37.167
-And the woman who wrote the fax
to us has come to the show

00:09:37.167 --> 00:09:39.333
and had a great time,
and we bought her a drink

00:09:39.333 --> 00:09:41.542
and showed her her own letter
up on the wall.

00:09:41.542 --> 00:09:44.042
And I like to think that was
a moving moment for her.

00:09:44.042 --> 00:09:45.500
-It was a moving moment for us.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.417
I really wanted to see
what she said,

00:09:47.417 --> 00:09:49.375
and it was nice
that she liked it.

00:09:49.375 --> 00:09:51.125
-That's amazing.
I love that.

00:09:51.125 --> 00:09:53.625
Seth, you spoke about
how much influence

00:09:53.625 --> 00:09:55.292
this had on your career.

00:09:55.292 --> 00:09:57.625
Can you talk about,
namely, how you heard of it?

00:09:57.625 --> 00:09:59.333
Like, how did it come
across your radar

00:09:59.333 --> 00:10:01.125
when you were graduating
from school?

00:10:01.125 --> 00:10:03.417
-Yeah, so, it was
real early days of the theater.

00:10:03.417 --> 00:10:06.042
I think it had only been 2 or 3
seasons that you guys had done,

00:10:06.042 --> 00:10:08.250
and it wasn't year-round
when I started.

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:12.208
But my dear college friend
and close friend today,

00:10:12.208 --> 00:10:15.125
a former "Late Night" writer,
Peter Grosz,

00:10:15.125 --> 00:10:17.208
he basically saw
an audition notice for it.

00:10:17.208 --> 00:10:18.875
He had talked to somebody
who'd done it.

00:10:18.875 --> 00:10:21.542
He was far more adventuresome
in spirit than I was

00:10:21.542 --> 00:10:23.417
and thought we should
go audition for it.

00:10:23.417 --> 00:10:25.292
And the other thing
I should note is that

00:10:25.292 --> 00:10:27.792
Pep and Andrew
both went to Northwestern,

00:10:27.792 --> 00:10:29.750
which is where Pete and I went.
Pep was in Mee-Ow,

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:32.958
which was the improv troupe at
Northwestern that I was also in.

00:10:32.958 --> 00:10:35.958
So, in a weird way, it was
a tailor-made audition

00:10:35.958 --> 00:10:39.500
for Pete and my skill set,
because you could tell,

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:40.958
"Oh, the person
who's putting this together

00:10:40.958 --> 00:10:43.042
has done Mee-Ow,
and we've done Mee-Ow."

00:10:43.042 --> 00:10:45.958
And so it was a course of three
days of auditioning for it,

00:10:45.958 --> 00:10:47.667
and it never...
In the back of my head,

00:10:47.667 --> 00:10:50.792
it just never occurred to me
that we might get the job,

00:10:50.792 --> 00:10:52.292
until we got the job.

00:10:52.292 --> 00:10:54.167
And then it was very funny

00:10:54.167 --> 00:10:55.958
because Pete was the one
who thought it was a good idea,

00:10:55.958 --> 00:10:57.708
and then immediately
he was like,

00:10:57.708 --> 00:11:00.458
"I got this girlfriend and I
don't know if it's a good idea."

00:11:00.458 --> 00:11:03.167
And I was all
guns blazing to go.

00:11:03.167 --> 00:11:04.625
In the end,
Pete went as well.

00:11:04.625 --> 00:11:06.292
But I didn't have
a passport at the time.

00:11:06.292 --> 00:11:07.750
I'd never left the country,

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:12.500
and so it was a giant
leap of faith

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:14.667
that was really,
I mean, incredible.

00:11:14.667 --> 00:11:16.333
It was incredibly rewarding.

00:11:16.333 --> 00:11:18.000
When I look back
at the bigger swings

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:19.917
I've taken in my life,
that one certainly counts.

00:11:19.917 --> 00:11:21.750
-Seth and Pete both had
a great audition.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.333
I remember they did a big --
they did a scene in the audition

00:11:24.333 --> 00:11:26.292
involving an enormous
cheese wheel, right?

00:11:26.292 --> 00:11:27.750
And they were rolling
a big cheese wheel.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.458
That, I do remember
from your audition.

00:11:29.458 --> 00:11:32.167
-I remember a big cowboy song
that Seth sang.

00:11:32.167 --> 00:11:33.583
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:11:33.583 --> 00:11:35.042
-Do you remember that, Seth?

00:11:35.042 --> 00:11:36.542
-I don't have
any memory of that.

00:11:36.542 --> 00:11:38.417
I know that I've been told
in the years since

00:11:38.417 --> 00:11:41.125
to stop it
with my cowboy songs.

00:11:41.125 --> 00:11:42.792
[ As Lorne Michaels ]
"You know, I don't see you

00:11:42.792 --> 00:11:44.667
as 'the cowboy song guy.'"

00:11:44.667 --> 00:11:45.917
I'm not gonna say
who said it.

00:11:45.917 --> 00:11:47.375
I'm not gonna
say who said it.

00:11:47.375 --> 00:11:49.417
-And then, Seth,
you quit your job, too.

00:11:49.417 --> 00:11:51.333
It was kind of similar to mine,
if I remember correctly.

00:11:51.333 --> 00:11:53.917
-Yeah, I said...
I think what it was in the end

00:11:53.917 --> 00:11:57.042
was they said, "You don't
actually have to quit.

00:11:57.042 --> 00:11:58.542
You just tell us you can't make

00:11:58.542 --> 00:12:01.208
your next year's worth
of shifts."

00:12:01.208 --> 00:12:02.875
At the Twisted Lizard.

00:12:02.875 --> 00:12:06.625
And I will say,
when I left Boom Chicago,

00:12:06.625 --> 00:12:11.125
I came back and started working
at the Twisted Lizard again.

00:12:11.125 --> 00:12:15.167
And that was what I like to call
the worst time of my life.

00:12:15.167 --> 00:12:18.500
[ Laughter ]

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:21.542
-In the book, the majority
of the alumni talk so lovingly

00:12:21.542 --> 00:12:23.917
about the Dutch national
football club

00:12:23.917 --> 00:12:26.125
and how many Boom alums
became diehard fans,

00:12:26.125 --> 00:12:28.708
including Brendan Hunt
and Jason Sudeikis, obviously,

00:12:28.708 --> 00:12:30.875
who created
and starred in "Ted Lasso."

00:12:30.875 --> 00:12:35.417
Can you guys talk about how that
took over in terms of the fandom

00:12:35.417 --> 00:12:38.250
and how you all became
such devoted supporters?

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:40.083
-This is really Saskia,
who's not here,

00:12:40.083 --> 00:12:44.958
which is Andrew's wife
and sort of the third leg

00:12:44.958 --> 00:12:47.750
of the tripod that keeps
Boom Chicago up and running.

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.458
But she is Dutch
and she loves football.

00:12:50.458 --> 00:12:53.667
And the first World Cup

00:12:53.667 --> 00:12:55.542
I was there for
was the 1998 World Cup.

00:12:55.542 --> 00:12:57.208
And the Dutch team
was very good,

00:12:57.208 --> 00:12:59.083
made it to the semifinals,
and it was quite dramatic.

00:12:59.083 --> 00:13:01.792
And you just watch the whole
city sort of turn orange

00:13:01.792 --> 00:13:05.417
and everybody's in a really
good mood all the time.

00:13:05.417 --> 00:13:07.500
And, uh, I love Dutch people,

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:10.917
but they're not in a really
good mood all the time.

00:13:10.917 --> 00:13:14.792
So, when that happens,
it's hard not to think like,

00:13:14.792 --> 00:13:16.625
"Ah, this is a wave
I want to ride."

00:13:16.625 --> 00:13:18.667
And it was just really fun.

00:13:18.667 --> 00:13:21.583
And mostly,
I think at that time, I mean,

00:13:21.583 --> 00:13:24.708
obviously it's different now,
but almost all of us were

00:13:24.708 --> 00:13:29.792
American sports fans who had not
fallen in love with soccer yet.

00:13:29.792 --> 00:13:31.875
And so that was kind
of our introduction

00:13:31.875 --> 00:13:33.333
to how wonderful it was.

00:13:33.333 --> 00:13:35.125
-Yeah, our theater
at the time, too,

00:13:35.125 --> 00:13:37.625
was on the Leidseplein,
which is a big square

00:13:37.625 --> 00:13:40.000
where a lot of people go
to watch these games.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.625
And when Ajax or when
the Dutch national team wins,

00:13:43.625 --> 00:13:46.667
there's a balcony in front
of the big theater there,

00:13:46.667 --> 00:13:50.333
and they raise the trophy

00:13:50.333 --> 00:13:52.125
on the -- in front
of thousands of fans.

00:13:52.125 --> 00:13:55.292
So, it was not only
a great season for football

00:13:55.292 --> 00:13:57.583
and a great set of years,

00:13:57.583 --> 00:14:00.500
it was also the place
where football was celebrated --

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:02.208
football meaning soccer.

00:14:02.208 --> 00:14:04.292
And that was an extra piece

00:14:04.292 --> 00:14:05.958
that the celebrations
were sort of

00:14:05.958 --> 00:14:07.792
part of the Boom Chicago
experience as well.

00:14:07.792 --> 00:14:09.375
-I would add just two things.

00:14:09.375 --> 00:14:11.833
One is Seth with you saying,
like, the whole city

00:14:11.833 --> 00:14:13.667
is in a good mood,
I would say yes,

00:14:13.667 --> 00:14:17.250
until the inevitable moment
that the Dutch team collapses,

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.625
and then it just --
it's so crushing.

00:14:19.625 --> 00:14:21.208
It's so miserable.
It's the worst.

00:14:21.208 --> 00:14:22.583
-Yeah.

00:14:22.583 --> 00:14:24.500
-And we've seen it live
once on...

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:26.458
-Well, not live, but we've
seen it on Seth's show,

00:14:26.458 --> 00:14:28.833
which is still one of my
favorite moments from the show,

00:14:28.833 --> 00:14:30.958
except that it involved
a Dutch team losing.

00:14:30.958 --> 00:14:33.458
-I rewatched it very recently,

00:14:33.458 --> 00:14:35.917
and it was very painful
and real.

00:14:35.917 --> 00:14:37.167
-Yeah, it was.

00:14:37.167 --> 00:14:38.958
-It was.
-It was drama on your show.

00:14:38.958 --> 00:14:40.375
-Was that when
you threw the iPad?

00:14:40.375 --> 00:14:41.625
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:14:41.625 --> 00:14:43.083
Okay, I remember that too. Yeah.

00:14:43.083 --> 00:14:45.583
-I'm very good
at not responding to people.

00:14:45.583 --> 00:14:47.750
I feel like a robust, happy life

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:50.750
is one that never responds
to people on social media.

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:52.708
But a few people, I remember,
after that --

00:14:52.708 --> 00:14:54.750
because I posted that video
on Twitter, they're like,

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:57.125
"Must be nice to be able
to break an iPad."

00:14:57.125 --> 00:14:59.833
And what I wanted to say is,

00:14:59.833 --> 00:15:02.667
"[Bleep] you!"

00:15:02.667 --> 00:15:05.083
-I'm not a sports fan
really at all, or I never was,

00:15:05.083 --> 00:15:06.875
and then I did the same thing
that you did, Seth,

00:15:06.875 --> 00:15:09.917
which was a show
with Brendan Hunt.

00:15:09.917 --> 00:15:12.542
But yours was only part
about football,

00:15:12.542 --> 00:15:15.417
but mine was totally about
the World Cup one year.

00:15:15.417 --> 00:15:17.917
And the bit was Brendan
knows everything

00:15:17.917 --> 00:15:19.625
about soccer, because he does,

00:15:19.625 --> 00:15:22.125
and I knew nothing about
anything, because I didn't.

00:15:22.125 --> 00:15:24.167
And we did this show
during the World Cup,

00:15:24.167 --> 00:15:26.542
and we would then show
the games after the show.

00:15:26.542 --> 00:15:29.917
I mean, the show was a flop,
because it turns out

00:15:29.917 --> 00:15:31.583
that if you're a football fan,
you don't really want to watch

00:15:31.583 --> 00:15:32.958
a comedy show,
and if you're a comedy show,

00:15:32.958 --> 00:15:34.667
you don't want to watch
the football.

00:15:34.667 --> 00:15:37.375
But I just learned a little bit
every day from Brendan

00:15:37.375 --> 00:15:40.375
from watching the games, and now
I can't miss a national game.

00:15:40.375 --> 00:15:42.250
-I should say,
a really funny thing,

00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:45.042
I think is very funny is that
these guys wanted to write --

00:15:45.042 --> 00:15:47.750
This book was originally written
at the 25th anniversary

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:49.000
and I think they thought,

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:50.125
"Oh, that's when you
write the book."

00:15:50.125 --> 00:15:51.667
Because at this point, like,

00:15:51.667 --> 00:15:53.958
it's not -- everything
great people have done,

00:15:53.958 --> 00:15:55.458
like, I feel like
they've done it.

00:15:55.458 --> 00:15:57.083
And then like,
the minute the book came out,

00:15:57.083 --> 00:15:59.833
"Ted Lasso" came out
and they were like, "[Bleep]."

00:15:59.833 --> 00:16:02.625
-Five years too early.
-Five years too early.

00:16:02.625 --> 00:16:04.333
So now this is --
this book is, and again,

00:16:04.333 --> 00:16:06.208
it's not
just Brendan and Jason.

00:16:06.208 --> 00:16:09.167
It's also Joe Kelly
who was out at Boom Chicago.

00:16:09.167 --> 00:16:12.833
And I should note, I think that
I should say, Jon and Andrew

00:16:12.833 --> 00:16:15.292
were going to be guests on
the show in a couple of weeks,

00:16:15.292 --> 00:16:17.333
and due to the fact
that we're not doing shows

00:16:17.333 --> 00:16:19.125
because of the writer's strike,
they will not be on.

00:16:19.125 --> 00:16:21.542
But I think you guys would have
been the, if you count me,

00:16:21.542 --> 00:16:23.667
you would have been the
11th and 12th Boom alum

00:16:23.667 --> 00:16:25.000
who has appeared on the show.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.000
-Wow.
-So can you name...

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.625
I'm going to give you me.
Can you name the other nine?

00:16:30.625 --> 00:16:32.000
-Oh, gosh.
-Are we counting, Pete?

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.125
-Yes. Pete counts.
Now you need eight.

00:16:34.125 --> 00:16:35.917
-Amber.
-Seven.

00:16:35.917 --> 00:16:37.875
-Tarik.
-Tarik.

00:16:37.875 --> 00:16:39.833
-Yeah, he's been
on the show. He did bits.

00:16:39.833 --> 00:16:41.458
-I forgot about Tarik.
I thought he was only Amber.

00:16:41.458 --> 00:16:43.667
So I'm going to give you --
You still have seven.

00:16:43.667 --> 00:16:46.208
-Okay, Jordan, of course.
-Yep. Six.

00:16:46.208 --> 00:16:49.542
-Ike, Kay, Brendan, Matt Jones.
-Five, four, three.

00:16:49.542 --> 00:16:51.875
-The other two are --
one is obvious.

00:16:51.875 --> 00:16:53.458
-Yeah, one is obvious
that you're missing.

00:16:53.458 --> 00:16:55.042
-Josh.
-Josh, yep.

00:16:55.042 --> 00:16:56.875
And one more.

00:16:56.875 --> 00:16:58.333
I'm going to give it to you.
Joe Kelly was on.

00:16:58.333 --> 00:16:59.708
-Joe Kelly. Yeah.

00:16:59.708 --> 00:17:01.208
-Also Carice was on.

00:17:01.208 --> 00:17:04.625
-She's not technically
Boom Chicago though.

00:17:04.625 --> 00:17:06.042
-Oh, okay.

00:17:06.042 --> 00:17:07.708
-She was just one of these
really, you know,

00:17:07.708 --> 00:17:08.958
the hanger-on type.

00:17:08.958 --> 00:17:10.625
We had a lot of Dutch celebs

00:17:10.625 --> 00:17:13.417
who just wanted to be
around the Boom Chicago heat.

00:17:13.417 --> 00:17:16.083
-It's so funny, she's become

00:17:16.083 --> 00:17:19.708
perhaps the leading female actor
in the Netherlands.

00:17:19.708 --> 00:17:22.042
And when we kind of say,
"Oh, yeah, she used to date Josh

00:17:22.042 --> 00:17:24.333
and hung out a lot
and, you know, would get high

00:17:24.333 --> 00:17:25.792
on the couches with us,"

00:17:25.792 --> 00:17:27.375
people are like,
"What, Carice van Houten?"

00:17:27.375 --> 00:17:29.958
Yeah, we've known her that long.
-Yeah, Carice van Houten

00:17:29.958 --> 00:17:31.208
of "Game of Thrones."

00:17:31.208 --> 00:17:32.500
-Melisandre in
"Game of Thrones."

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.583
-Yes.
-I went to Burger King

00:17:34.583 --> 00:17:36.167
once with her and Josh,
and we were super high.

00:17:36.167 --> 00:17:38.167
I'm not sure if this
is a good story to tell.

00:17:38.167 --> 00:17:40.625
And we started
doing bits with each other

00:17:40.625 --> 00:17:42.250
and our thing became,

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:44.625
"Oh, we don't understand
each other when we do bits."

00:17:44.625 --> 00:17:47.375
And that was our relationship
for a long time.

00:17:47.375 --> 00:17:49.333
-One of my favorite things,
she says in the book

00:17:49.333 --> 00:17:51.292
is how she had, like,

00:17:51.292 --> 00:17:53.792
Dutch people don't date
the way Americans date.

00:17:53.792 --> 00:17:57.000
And so Josh was sort of
her first boyfriend

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:02.625
in the American sense,
hand-holding, flowers.

00:18:02.625 --> 00:18:04.500
And then one night
they both got high

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:06.875
and were watching
some documentary

00:18:06.875 --> 00:18:08.875
about a American serial killer.

00:18:08.875 --> 00:18:10.708
And she was so stoned
she thought,

00:18:10.708 --> 00:18:12.583
"Oh, no, it's Josh,"

00:18:12.583 --> 00:18:14.667
because it was all...

00:18:14.667 --> 00:18:17.000
it was all one
of those like, "Dateline", like,

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:20.125
"It was the love
that was too good to be true."

00:18:20.125 --> 00:18:22.667
She was like, "Oh my God, this
guy did everything Josh did,

00:18:22.667 --> 00:18:25.083
flowers, hand-holding.

00:18:25.083 --> 00:18:28.458
It's all the classic signs."
[ Laughter ]

00:18:28.458 --> 00:18:31.125
-I think that's a good segue
to ask Andrew and Pep,

00:18:31.125 --> 00:18:33.083
this community of actors
and performers

00:18:33.083 --> 00:18:35.000
who developed this love
for Dutch culture,

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:36.917
including the Dutch
national team, too,

00:18:36.917 --> 00:18:38.917
but what do you think
are the most common things

00:18:38.917 --> 00:18:41.500
that maybe stay
with Boom Chicago alumni,

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:44.917
in terms of the connection to
Amsterdam or love for country?

00:18:44.917 --> 00:18:47.417
What do you hear most
from people that they miss

00:18:47.417 --> 00:18:48.750
or that they want to
come back to?

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:50.042
-Hmm.

00:18:50.042 --> 00:18:52.000
I mean,
it might be the Efteling.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:54.458
-The Efteling is a great
amusement park

00:18:54.458 --> 00:18:56.000
in the Netherlands.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:57.958
It's kind of the Dollywood
of the Netherlands,

00:18:57.958 --> 00:19:00.458
which is, it's super credible
and a world-class park

00:19:00.458 --> 00:19:02.000
that's a little bit smaller.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:03.917
It's in nature and it's --

00:19:03.917 --> 00:19:05.417
it's a little bit
more regional-feeling.

00:19:05.417 --> 00:19:07.333
But everyone loves the Efteling.

00:19:07.333 --> 00:19:09.125
-I didn't realize,
though, I read --

00:19:09.125 --> 00:19:11.250
and you will know
if this is true or not, Andrew,

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:15.083
the second most-attended
European amusement park

00:19:15.083 --> 00:19:18.000
to a Disneyland Paris?

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:19.500
Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:21.167
-Euro Disney, right?

00:19:21.167 --> 00:19:23.167
So it's small,
but it's real deal.

00:19:23.167 --> 00:19:24.875
-Oh, yeah. I'm sorry.

00:19:24.875 --> 00:19:27.292
It's only small compared
to Disney World,

00:19:27.292 --> 00:19:28.750
but it's certainly
the real deal.

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:30.708
-To me, the thing about
the Efteling,

00:19:30.708 --> 00:19:32.167
and it's such a Dutch thing
about it,

00:19:32.167 --> 00:19:34.542
is that whereas most
American amusement parks,

00:19:34.542 --> 00:19:36.125
they sort of tear down
everything

00:19:36.125 --> 00:19:38.375
and then build the park,
here, there was this forest,

00:19:38.375 --> 00:19:41.208
and they left the forest
and sort of build around it,

00:19:41.208 --> 00:19:42.958
and it's just this gorgeous,

00:19:42.958 --> 00:19:46.250
yeah, park with awesome rides
in there as well.

00:19:46.250 --> 00:19:48.417
-I think alumni
also miss bike riding,

00:19:48.417 --> 00:19:50.542
because that is really
a part of Amsterdam is,

00:19:50.542 --> 00:19:51.917
you just bike everywhere

00:19:51.917 --> 00:19:53.875
and it's just your way
you get around.

00:19:53.875 --> 00:19:55.375
I think that's nice.

00:19:55.375 --> 00:19:57.458
Even though the weather
isn't always great,

00:19:57.458 --> 00:20:00.000
there's certainly a cafe,

00:19:57.458 --> 00:20:00.000
there's certainly a cafe,

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.208
terrace outdoor culture,
which is nice.

00:20:02.208 --> 00:20:04.917
And when the weather is good,
everyone just enjoys it.

00:20:04.917 --> 00:20:07.000
-When the weather's good,
somehow nobody's employed

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:09.208
and everybody is sitting
on a terrace drinking rosé.

00:20:09.208 --> 00:20:11.625
It's really weird. I don't know
what their jobs are.

00:20:11.625 --> 00:20:13.750
I know what mine is,
and it allows me to drink rosé

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:15.417
at 2 p.m. on a Tuesday,

00:20:15.417 --> 00:20:17.333
but I don't know about
everybody else. It's weird.

00:20:17.333 --> 00:20:18.875
-It does, I felt like, the city

00:20:18.875 --> 00:20:22.458
always expanded to your mood
and the weather,

00:20:22.458 --> 00:20:24.833
and if you were having
a good time,

00:20:24.833 --> 00:20:27.083
like, that city stays open late

00:20:27.083 --> 00:20:30.917
in a way that is
not aggressive or grimy.

00:20:30.917 --> 00:20:32.458
You can just have --

00:20:32.458 --> 00:20:34.250
I mean, I feel like I've never

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:38.250
had more 20-hour sort of,
you know, party days

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:39.917
where you just,
the day starts and then

00:20:39.917 --> 00:20:41.292
the next thing you know,
it's 5:00 in the morning.

00:20:41.292 --> 00:20:42.958
And, you know,
especially in the summer

00:20:42.958 --> 00:20:45.208
when it's already
getting light again.

00:20:45.208 --> 00:20:47.375
I remember one of the first,
and to this day,

00:20:47.375 --> 00:20:49.042
maybe the coolest thing
Pep ever did.

00:20:49.042 --> 00:20:52.375
We were on a boat ride,
and remember, there was a place,

00:20:52.375 --> 00:20:54.333
a bakery you could
pull the boat up next to

00:20:54.333 --> 00:20:57.167
and get like,
the first croissants of the day?

00:20:57.167 --> 00:20:59.292
And it was really
just amazing.

00:20:59.292 --> 00:21:00.875
I remember that
was like, Pete and I,

00:21:00.875 --> 00:21:02.833
our introduction to Amsterdam
was let's go on

00:21:02.833 --> 00:21:05.708
a midnight or whatever,
probably 3:00 a.m. boat ride,

00:21:05.708 --> 00:21:07.083
and the next thing we know,
we're pulling up,

00:21:07.083 --> 00:21:08.500
like, the window onto the canal

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:10.000
and somebody just
hands out a bag.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.417
-He'd sneak out
with his undershirt on.

00:21:12.417 --> 00:21:16.875
-Yeah, it was certainly
the most like a drug deal

00:21:16.875 --> 00:21:19.375
I've ever seen
purchasing croissants.

00:21:19.375 --> 00:21:21.875
[ Laughter ]

00:21:21.875 --> 00:21:25.042
-Did you guys look along
the way at any point,

00:21:25.042 --> 00:21:26.792
maybe when you look up
at that fax

00:21:26.792 --> 00:21:28.750
from that Dutch woman
in the government,

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:32.292
did you ever think this result
was possible 30 years later?

00:21:32.292 --> 00:21:34.583
To have this be your life,
that you've created this thing

00:21:34.583 --> 00:21:37.167
that is obviously
such a success?

00:21:37.167 --> 00:21:39.375
-What I always say is,
I think I did not.

00:21:39.375 --> 00:21:41.500
I thought, "Wow, this is
going to be a fantastic

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:43.500
summer project in 1993."

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:46.333
And I think, deep down,
Andrew thought the opposite.

00:21:46.333 --> 00:21:48.167
-Yeah, I thought
it was going to work.

00:21:48.167 --> 00:21:50.625
Yeah,
I thought it was going to work.

00:21:50.625 --> 00:21:53.833
What I did not predict is that
I would be middle-aged one day.

00:21:53.833 --> 00:21:56.083
That was inconceivable.

00:21:56.083 --> 00:21:58.000
-I will say that I studied
abroad in college

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.458
and the thing to do
was go to Amsterdam

00:22:00.458 --> 00:22:03.667
and obviously eat space cakes,
but go see Boom Chicago.

00:22:03.667 --> 00:22:05.333
And that was in the earlier,

00:22:05.333 --> 00:22:07.375
maybe, like,
right after Seth left.

00:22:07.375 --> 00:22:10.625
But like, early days of 2000,
and my friends were all doing

00:22:10.625 --> 00:22:12.083
that when they went
to Amsterdam.

00:22:12.083 --> 00:22:14.917
That's pretty fantastic.
-We like your friends.

00:22:14.917 --> 00:22:16.875
-I wanted to ask you guys,
all three of you,

00:22:16.875 --> 00:22:20.042
many of the stories in the book,
there's so many funny anecdotes.

00:22:20.042 --> 00:22:22.458
Some include drugs
and some capacity,

00:22:22.458 --> 00:22:24.792
and I'm sure there are some
that maybe were inappropriate

00:22:24.792 --> 00:22:26.583
for the book on some level.

00:22:26.583 --> 00:22:28.333
But was there anything that you
really wished made the cut

00:22:28.333 --> 00:22:31.667
that you either couldn't keep in
for specific reasons

00:22:31.667 --> 00:22:34.042
or just didn't have
the space to have in there?

00:22:34.042 --> 00:22:36.708
-The editor's cut.
-[ Laughs ]

00:22:36.708 --> 00:22:40.458
-There was a whole chapter
written in The Voice of Germany,

00:22:40.458 --> 00:22:42.542
and it was hilarious
and it was cut.

00:22:42.542 --> 00:22:45.042
It was an awesome chapter.

00:22:45.042 --> 00:22:47.375
That was my darling
that was cut.

00:22:47.375 --> 00:22:49.542
Seth, I'm going to send you
a copy of the Germany chapter.

00:22:49.542 --> 00:22:51.042
-I don't know
if it's in the book,

00:22:51.042 --> 00:22:52.792
but we were talking
about the Efteling,

00:22:52.792 --> 00:22:55.750
and this was one of my favorite
"Shame on Ike" stories,

00:22:55.750 --> 00:22:58.500
of which there are legion.

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:02.250
But somebody brought a football,
like, an American football,

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:05.542
which is already just
a terrifying shape

00:23:05.542 --> 00:23:06.833
to Dutch people.

00:23:06.833 --> 00:23:08.500
And we were at Efteling,
and we're kind of

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:09.917
throwing the football
back and forth.

00:23:09.917 --> 00:23:11.208
And one of my favorite
things about Ike

00:23:11.208 --> 00:23:13.250
is he looks athletic
and he's not.

00:23:13.250 --> 00:23:17.125
And so he went
to throw the ball to somebody,

00:23:17.125 --> 00:23:19.375
and he threw the worst pass
I've ever seen.

00:23:19.375 --> 00:23:22.167
It went flying, and it was
going towards an old woman

00:23:22.167 --> 00:23:24.583
in a wheelchair who was just...

00:23:24.583 --> 00:23:27.083
And two things happened.

00:23:27.083 --> 00:23:29.292
Ike threw it,
saw what was going to happen,

00:23:29.292 --> 00:23:30.750
turned his head
and just walked away

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:32.750
like he hadn't
even been playing football.

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:36.417
Like, he immediately was
building his own alibi.

00:23:36.417 --> 00:23:38.375
And my brother, God love him,

00:23:38.375 --> 00:23:40.583
who is the most important thing
that ever happened to Ike

00:23:40.583 --> 00:23:42.083
is that he met
someone like my brother.

00:23:42.083 --> 00:23:45.333
Josh just went in
a dead sprint and dove

00:23:45.333 --> 00:23:46.750
and just tapped the ball away.

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:47.917
Didn't catch it,
but just, like,

00:23:47.917 --> 00:23:49.250
tapped it away from hitting this

00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:50.833
Dutch woman right in the face.

00:23:50.833 --> 00:23:52.958
And then the whole bus
ride home, I was like,

00:23:52.958 --> 00:23:55.125
"You just walked away."

00:23:55.125 --> 00:23:57.500
He was like, "See,
I don't wanna -- I -- Look.

00:23:57.500 --> 00:23:58.792
We could talk about this
as much as you want.

00:23:58.792 --> 00:24:00.917
That's not how I saw it."
[ Laughter ]

00:24:00.917 --> 00:24:03.458
-"'Cause I couldn't see it at
all because I was walking away

00:24:03.458 --> 00:24:05.083
and not looking."
-Yeah, "I don't know.

00:24:05.083 --> 00:24:07.167
-"Does everybody see
where their ball goes?"

00:24:07.167 --> 00:24:09.625
[ Laughter ]
-So in addition to the book

00:24:09.625 --> 00:24:11.750
coming out in July,
you guys are coming to the US.

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:13.542
You have a couple of events.

00:24:13.542 --> 00:24:15.250
Can you tell us about those
and where people can find you?

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:17.792
-Yes, absolutely.
We've got three big stops

00:24:17.792 --> 00:24:20.083
with nice events.
They're not just book readings.

00:24:20.083 --> 00:24:23.958
They're things with performances
and a lot of alumni as well.

00:24:23.958 --> 00:24:27.292
We start in New York,
June 21st at Le Poisson Rouge.

00:24:27.292 --> 00:24:30.208
Seth is going to host it,
Amber Ruffin, Tarik Davis,

00:24:30.208 --> 00:24:34.167
Holly Walker, other alumni
who are based in New York.

00:24:34.167 --> 00:24:38.625
Then the next day, Chicago
on the 22nd of June at iO.

00:24:38.625 --> 00:24:40.833
Pep and I are going
to be guests,

00:24:40.833 --> 00:24:44.833
monologuists telling stories
at the Armando Diaz.

00:24:44.833 --> 00:24:47.625
And then in Los Angeles
on the 29th

00:24:47.625 --> 00:24:49.875
at Gold-Diggers
in East Hollywood,

00:24:49.875 --> 00:24:54.292
Ike Barinholtz will
be hosting Colton Dunn, others.

00:24:54.292 --> 00:24:55.917
Brendan Hunt
will be performing.

00:24:55.917 --> 00:24:57.333
-That's going to be
a crazy show, too.

00:24:57.333 --> 00:24:59.083
-Yeah.
In his band character,

00:24:59.083 --> 00:25:00.625
Elvis Prestello.

00:25:00.625 --> 00:25:03.333
That has not happened in years,

00:25:03.333 --> 00:25:07.000
so that's a big event
at Gold-Diggers on the 29th.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.833
Hope we see people there.
-Yeah, it'll be great.

00:25:09.833 --> 00:25:11.542
-Well, congratulations
on this book, you guys.

00:25:11.542 --> 00:25:12.833
It was so much fun to read,

00:25:12.833 --> 00:25:14.792
and it will be published
in early July,

00:25:14.792 --> 00:25:17.167
but it is available
for preorder now.

00:25:17.167 --> 00:25:18.875
So everyone go do that.

00:25:18.875 --> 00:25:22.167
And Seth, Pep, Andrew,
thanks so much for your time.

00:25:22.167 --> 00:25:24.083
-Thank you.
-Thanks a lot.

00:25:24.083 --> 00:25:25.083
-Thanks, Jenks.

00:25:25.083 --> 00:25:27.833
♪♪

